<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ariq Böke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1219">1219</a>–<a href="/years/1266">1266</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ariq Böke was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and the youngest brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> and Kublai. After Möngke's death in 1259 he claimed the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> with support in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, while Kublai claimed it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Kublai defeated him in the civil war that followed, securing the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariq-boke.jpg" alt="Ariq Böke" width="290" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miskin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ariq_B%C3%B6ke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ariq Böke was the seventh and youngest son of Tolui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki">Sorghaghtani Beki</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> died in 1227, leadership passed to Ögedei, and Ariq Böke peacefully attended the elections of both Ögedei and Ögedei's son Güyük. His eldest brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> was enthroned in 1250, which made the family more powerful among the descendants of Genghis.</p><p>His mother was Christian, and he was known for sympathy towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The Franciscan William of Rubruck, an envoy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, wrote that he saw Ariq Böke make the sign of the cross and say that the Messiah is God. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> arranged a Confucian tutor for him, but Ariq Böke did not take to Chinese scholars as Kublai had.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>While <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> and Kublai conquered Southern China and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> advanced west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, the affairs of the Mongol heartland were left in Ariq Böke's hands. When Möngke died in 1259, Ariq Böke was elected khan in the absence of his brothers, with the support of most of the ministers and powerful families at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>, including Möngke's family, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a>. In May 1260 Kublai was elected khan by his own supporters, and a civil war broke out between the brothers.</p><p>Ariq Böke had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>'s candidate Abishqa, who was loyal to Kublai, captured and killed, and installed his own ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alghu">Alghu</a>. Alghu later deserted him and killed his envoys, but Ariq Böke defeated him in a second engagement and forced him to flee west. Kublai, however, commanded Mongol cavalry and Mongolian, Manchurian, Han, Kipchak and Goryeo troops, and he blockaded trade from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> to cut the food supply. His supporter Kadan crushed Ariq Böke's force under General Alandar, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> twice slipped from Ariq Böke's control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ariq Böke submitted to Kublai in 1264 and was imprisoned. He died a few years later in unclear circumstances, which gave rise to rumours of poisoning. The scholar David Morgan sees him as the figurehead of a school of thought among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> that opposed Kublai's attraction to settled civilisation and the Chinese way of life. In the traditionalist view the Mongol centre should remain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and nomadic life be preserved, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> merely exploited. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a> continued this tradition. Some of Ariq Böke's descendants became important in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and the Northern Yuan dynasty, and Yesüder, khan from 1388, can be traced back to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ariq Böke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That changed when Alghu (r. 1260–1266) used the civil war between Kublai and <b>Ariq Böke</b> to assert real independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the succession war between Kublai and <b>Ariq Böke</b> (1260–1264), the Golden Horde, Ilkhanate, Chagatai Khanate and Yuan dynasty operated as largely independent states, though they continued to acknowledge a shared Chinggisid heritage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Kublai Khan besieged Ezhou, but a coming civil war with his brother <b>Ariq Böke</b> drew him north, and the Song pushed his forces back across the Yangtze.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The tension fed the Toluid Civil War with his younger brother <b>Ariq Böke</b> (1260–1264) and the long conflict with his kinsman Kaidu in Central Asia, which helped divide the Mongol world into rival khanates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…left the Empire with two absentee kings during what historians call the Great Interregnum; and after the death of the Great Khan Möngke in 1259 rival kurultais elected his brothers Kublai Khan and <b>Ariq Böke</b>, whose war, the Toluid Civil War, hastened the division of the Mongol Empire.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>The deaths of Ögedei in 1241 and Möngke in 1259 called princes home to choose a successor, and in 1260 rival kurultais elected Kublai Khan and his brother <b>Ariq Böke</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
